    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.10",
    "topic_title": "Origins of Cell Compartmentalization",
    "stem": "A student is writing an explanation of how cell compartmentalization arose and how it differs between cell types. Which statement correctly describes both the origin of mitochondria and chloroplasts and the difference in compartmentalization between prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "Mitochondria and chloroplasts descended from once free-living prokaryotes engulfed by a host cell; prokaryotes have no internal organization, so all their molecules and reactions are mixed uniformly in the cytoplasm, while eukaryotes partition the cell with internal membranes.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This overstates the contrast. It treats having no membrane-bound organelles as having no internal organization at all. Prokaryotes still have specialized internal regions even though those regions are not enclosed by membranes. Fix: When contrasting cell types, ask whether a statement says prokaryotes lack membrane-bound organelles or wrongly says they lack all internal organization."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "Mitochondria and chloroplasts formed when the host cell's plasma membrane folded inward around part of its own cytoplasm, which explains their double membranes; prokaryotes have specialized internal regions, while eukaryotes partition the cell with internal membranes.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This confuses inward folding of the host's own membrane with endosymbiosis. Endosymbiotic theory says these organelles came from separate, once free-living prokaryotic cells that were engulfed, not from pieces of the host's own cytoplasm. Fix: Check that an origin statement for mitochondria and chloroplasts names once free-living prokaryotes as their ancestors."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "Because mitochondria and chloroplasts descended from free-living prokaryotes, modern prokaryotic cells also contain mitochondria and chloroplasts, so both cell types partition their interiors with membrane-bound organelles.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This mixes up evolutionary ancestry with present-day structure. Mitochondria and chloroplasts descended from ancestral prokaryotes, but modern prokaryotes typically lack membrane-bound organelles. Their specialized internal regions are not membrane-enclosed. Fix: Separate what an organelle evolved from and what modern cells contain, and check whether each claim is about the ancestors or about present-day cells."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "Mitochondria and chloroplasts descended from once free-living prokaryotes that were engulfed by a host cell, which fits their double membranes; prokaryotes typically lack membrane-bound organelles but have specialized internal regions, while eukaryotes partition the cell with internal membranes.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "This states the endosymbiotic origin from free-living prokaryotes and backs it with evidence: a double membrane fits an engulfed cell's own membrane plus the host's surrounding membrane. It also contrasts the cell types accurately. Prokaryotes have specialized regions without membrane-bound organelles, and eukaryotes use internal membranes to separate specialized regions."
     }
    ]
   },
