   "item": {
    "subject_key": "biology",
    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.10",
    "topic_title": "Origins of Cell Compartmentalization",
    "stem": "Mitochondria and chloroplasts are each surrounded by two membranes. Which statement best explains their evolutionary origin and accurately compares compartmentalization in prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "Mitochondria and chloroplasts evolved solely from membrane folds within ancestral host cells. Prokaryotes typically have specialized internal regions without membrane-bound organelles, whereas eukaryotic internal membranes partition specialized regions.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "The membrane-folding trap substitutes development of the host's own membranes for endosymbiotic origin. Mitochondria and chloroplasts evolved from formerly free-living prokaryotes, not solely from host membrane folds. Fix: Check whether the explanation identifies a formerly free-living prokaryote as the ancestor of each organelle."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "Mitochondria and chloroplasts evolved from free-living prokaryotes through endosymbiosis. Prokaryotes lack all specialized internal organization, whereas eukaryotic internal membranes partition specialized regions.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "The unorganized-prokaryote trap equates lacking membrane-bound organelles with lacking internal organization. Prokaryotes typically lack those organelles but still have specialized internal regions. Fix: Separate two questions: does the cell have specialized internal regions, and are those regions enclosed by membranes?"
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "Mitochondria and chloroplasts evolved from free-living prokaryotes through endosymbiosis. Prokaryotes typically contain membrane-bound mitochondria and chloroplasts, whereas eukaryotic internal membranes partition specialized regions.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "The ancestor–organelle trap confuses prokaryotic ancestors with the organelles that evolved from them. A prokaryotic origin does not mean prokaryotes typically contain mitochondria or chloroplasts; they typically lack membrane-bound organelles. Fix: Distinguish the ancestral free-living cell from the organelle it became inside a host cell."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "Mitochondria and chloroplasts evolved from free-living prokaryotes through endosymbiosis. Prokaryotes typically have specialized internal regions without membrane-bound organelles, whereas eukaryotic internal membranes partition specialized regions.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Endosymbiosis explains how formerly free-living prokaryotes became organelles within host cells; double membranes are consistent with this origin. The comparison distinguishes internal organization from membrane-bound compartmentalization: prokaryotes have specialized regions, while eukaryotes partition regions with internal membranes."
     }
    ]
